/* data/learn-c/phy-12-05.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-05.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-05"] = {"5.1":{"s":"Oscillation means moving to and fro about a fixed position: a swing, a clock pendulum, a guitar string, a tuning fork. This chapter builds the maths of the simplest kind, simple harmonic motion, and then looks at pendulums, damping and resonance.","w":["Periodic does not always mean simple harmonic. SHM needs a restoring force proportional to the displacement.","The period does not depend on the amplitude in SHM."],"x":["Oscillation means moving to and fro about a fixed position. A child on a swing, the bob of a wall clock, a plucked guitar string, the piston in an engine and the atoms in a solid all oscillate.","The simplest and most important kind is simple harmonic motion, S.H.M. Almost any small vibration about a stable position turns out to be S.H.M. to a good approximation, which is why the same equations describe springs, pendulums, sound waves and AC circuits.","In this chapter we learn the definition of S.H.M., its equations, the energy, the spring and the pendulum, and then what happens when there is friction or an outside driving force."],"e":"A mass hanging on a spring, pulled down a little and let go, bobs up and down. A pendulum pulled a little to one side swings back and forth. Both are S.H.M. for small amplitudes."},"5.2":{"s":"Periodic motion repeats itself after equal intervals of time. Oscillation is periodic motion back and forth about a mean position. Every oscillation is periodic, but not every periodic motion (like the Earth going round the Sun) is an oscillation.","f":[{"x":"T = 2π/ω = 1/f","n":"Time period and frequency"}],"w":["Every oscillation is periodic, but a periodic motion (a planet's orbit) need not be an oscillation."],"x":["A motion that repeats itself after equal intervals of time is periodic. The time for one repeat is the period T. The number of repeats per second is the frequency n = 1/T, in hertz.","Not every periodic motion is an oscillation. The Earth going round the Sun repeats every year, but it does not move to and fro. An oscillation is a periodic motion in which the body moves back and forth about a mean (equilibrium) position.","A force that always points to the mean position, called a restoring force, is needed for oscillation. If a body is displaced from its equilibrium, the restoring force pulls it back. Because of its inertia, it overshoots, and then the force pulls it back from the other side. That is how the to-and-fro motion goes on."],"e":"A ball rolling in and out of a bowl oscillates about the bottom of the bowl. The bottom is the mean position, and the slope of the bowl provides the restoring force."},"5.3":{"s":"Linear S.H.M.: the restoring force always points to the mean position and is proportional to the displacement, f = −kx. The constant k is the force constant. The minus sign means force and displacement are in opposite directions. A block on a spring on a smooth table is the standard example.","f":[{"x":"f = -k*x","n":"Restoring force in linear S.H.M.; k = force constant, x = displacement from the mean position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.3, p.110"},{"x":"a = -(k/m)*x","n":"Acceleration: proportional to the displacement and towards the mean position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.3, p.110"}],"w":["The minus sign is the whole point: force and displacement are always opposite."],"x":["In linear simple harmonic motion the body moves along a straight line, and the restoring force F is proportional to the displacement x from the mean position and opposite to it: F = −kx. The constant k is the force constant, in N/m.","Since F = ma, the acceleration is a = −(k/m)x. So the acceleration is proportional to the displacement and always points towards the mean position. This is the test for S.H.M.: if a = −(a positive constant) × x, the motion is simple harmonic.","The constant k/m is written ω², so a = −ω²x. The quantity ω is the angular frequency, in rad/s, and the period is T = 2π/ω = 2π√(m/k).","A mass on a spring (T = 2π√(m/k)) and a simple pendulum (T = 2π√(L/g)) are the two standard examples. The period of the spring depends on the mass and stiffness, not on the amplitude."],"e":"A body of mass 0.5 kg on a spring with k = 200 N/m has T = 2π√(0.5/200) = 0.314 s."},"5.4":{"s":"Putting f = −kx into Newton's law gives the differential equation of S.H.M.: d²x/dt² + ω²x = 0, where ω² = k/m. Any motion that obeys this equation is simple harmonic. ω is called the angular frequency.","f":[{"x":"d^2x/dt^2 + omega^2*x = 0","n":"Differential equation of linear S.H.M.; omega^2 = k/m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.4, p.112"}],"w":["The equation has a plus sign: the acceleration is −ω² times x. A wrong sign gives growth, not oscillation.","Here ω is the angular frequency of the oscillation, not the angular velocity of a rotating body."],"x":["Put the restoring force F = −kx into Newton's second law, m d²x/dt² = −kx. Rearranged, this is d²x/dt² + ω²x = 0, with ω² = k/m. This is the differential equation of S.H.M.","It says that the second derivative of x (the acceleration) is a negative multiple of x itself. Only sine and cosine functions behave like that: differentiate x = A sin(ωt + α) twice and you get −ω² times x again. So x = A sin(ωt + α), or the same with cosine, is the solution.","A and α are two constants fixed by how the motion starts (the initial position and velocity). The value of ω is fixed by the system itself, through k and m. That is why the period of a spring does not depend on how far you pull it."],"e":"If x = 0.1 sin(10t), then ω = 10 rad/s, and d²x/dt² = −10² × 0.1 sin(10t) = −100x, so the equation d²x/dt² + 100x = 0 holds."},"5.5":{"s":"Solving the equation gives x = A sin(ωt + α). Velocity is v = ω√(A² − x²): largest at the mean position and zero at the extremes. Acceleration is a = −ω²x: zero at the mean position and largest at the extremes. A is the amplitude.","f":[{"x":"x = A*sin(omega*t + phi)","n":"Displacement; A = amplitude, phi = initial phase. Starting from an extreme, x = A cos(omega t).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.5, p.113"},{"x":"v = omega*sqrt(A^2 - x^2)","n":"Speed at displacement x; v_max = A*omega at the mean position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.5, p.112"},{"x":"a = -omega^2*x","n":"Acceleration; largest, omega^2 A, at the extremes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.5, p.112"}],"w":["At the mean position speed is largest and acceleration zero; at the extremes it is the other way round."],"x":["Take x = A sin(ωt + α). Differentiating once gives the velocity v = Aω cos(ωt + α). Differentiating again gives the acceleration a = −Aω² sin(ωt + α) = −ω²x.","The velocity can also be written in terms of position: v = ω√(A² − x²). It is largest, Aω, at the mean position (x = 0) and zero at the extremes (x = ±A).","The acceleration is zero at the mean position and largest, Aω², at the extremes, where it points back to the centre. The restoring force follows the acceleration.","So at the mean position the body moves fastest and feels no force. At the extreme position it stops for an instant and feels the largest force. Velocity leads displacement by a quarter of a cycle, and acceleration is opposite to displacement."],"e":"A = 0.1 m and ω = 10 rad/s. v_max = Aω = 1 m/s at the centre, and a_max = Aω² = 10 m/s² at the ends. At x = 0.06 m, v = 10√(0.01 − 0.0036) = 0.8 m/s."},"5.6":{"s":"The amplitude A is the largest displacement from the mean position. The period T is the time for one full oscillation, and the frequency n = 1/T is the number of oscillations per second. For a mass on a spring, a heavier mass gives a longer period and a stiffer spring a shorter one, whatever the amplitude. Springs joined end to end make a softer combination; springs side by side make a stiffer one.","f":[{"x":"T = 2*pi/omega","n":"Period of S.H.M.; omega = angular frequency, n = 1/T = omega/(2*pi).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.115"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(m/k)","n":"Mass m on a spring of force constant k.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.115"},{"x":"n = (1/(2*pi))*sqrt(k/m)","n":"Frequency of the same system.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.115"},{"x":"1/k_s = 1/k_1 + 1/k_2","n":"Two springs in series: effective force constant k_s (less than either).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.116"},{"x":"k_p = k_1 + k_2","n":"Two springs in parallel: effective force constant k_p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.116"}],"w":["The period depends on mass and force constant, not on the amplitude.","Series springs: the combined k is smaller than each. Parallel springs: the k's add."],"x":["The amplitude A is the largest distance from the mean position. It tells how big the oscillation is. The full path from one extreme to the other is 2A, and in one complete oscillation the body travels a distance 4A.","The period T is the time for one complete oscillation, and the frequency n = 1/T is the number of oscillations per second. They are linked to the angular frequency by ω = 2πn = 2π/T.","In true S.H.M. the period does not depend on the amplitude. This property is called isochronism, and it is why a pendulum clock keeps good time even when the swing dies down slowly. The period depends only on the system: T = 2π√(m/k) for a spring, and T = 2π√(L/g) for a pendulum.","The amplitude depends on how much energy you put in: the total energy is ½kA², so doubling the amplitude gives four times the energy."],"e":"A particle with x = 5 sin(4πt) cm has A = 5 cm, ω = 4π rad/s, n = 2 Hz and T = 0.5 s."},"5.7":{"s":"Reference circle: if a point moves uniformly on a circle, its shadow (projection) on a diameter performs S.H.M. The circle's radius is the amplitude and its angular speed is ω. This gives an easy way to picture and derive the S.H.M. equations.","w":["The shadow of uniform circular motion on ANY diameter is S.H.M.; the circle's radius is the amplitude."],"x":["There is a neat link between circular motion and S.H.M. Imagine a point P moving round a circle of radius A with constant angular speed ω. Shine a light from the side and look at the shadow of P on a diameter, which is its projection onto a straight line.","If the angle is ωt + α measured from a suitable line, the position of the shadow is x = A sin(ωt + α). That is exactly S.H.M., with amplitude equal to the radius of the circle and the same ω. This circle is called the reference circle.","So S.H.M. is the shadow of uniform circular motion. The speed of P is Aω, and the speed of the shadow is the part of it along the diameter, which gives v = Aω cos(ωt + α). The acceleration of P is Aω² towards the centre, and the shadow gets the component −ω²x.","This method makes many S.H.M. problems easy, because you can use the geometry of the circle (angles, phase) in place of algebra."],"e":"P goes round a circle of radius 0.2 m once every 2 s. Its shadow on a diameter performs S.H.M. with A = 0.2 m, T = 2 s and ω = π rad/s."},"5.8":{"s":"Phase tells the state of oscillation at an instant: where the particle is, which way it is moving, and in which oscillation. Position alone is not enough, because the particle passes the same point moving both ways. Phase is written as the angle (ωt + α); α is the initial phase (epoch).","f":[{"x":"x = A sin(ωt + φ)","n":"Displacement in S.H.M.. φ is the initial phase."},{"x":"Velocity leads displacement by π/2 ; acceleration is π out of phase with it","n":"Phase relation"}],"w":["Position alone does not fix the phase: the particle passes each point twice per cycle, moving opposite ways."],"x":["Phase tells the state of an oscillator at a given moment: where it is and which way it is moving. For x = A sin(ωt + α), the angle φ = ωt + α is the phase. The constant α is the initial phase, the phase at t = 0.","One full cycle is a change of phase of 2π rad (360°). Two bodies with the same phase are in step (in phase). If the phases differ by π, one is at the positive extreme when the other is at the negative extreme (opposite phase).","The phase difference between two S.H.M.s of the same frequency is the difference of their phase angles, and it stays constant in time. A phase difference of Δφ is the same as a time shift of Δt = Δφ/ω.","Within one S.H.M., the velocity is ahead of the displacement by π/2, and the acceleration is ahead by π (that is, opposite to the displacement)."],"e":"x₁ = sin(ωt) and x₂ = cos(ωt) = sin(ωt + π/2). Their phase difference is π/2, so x₂ is a quarter of a period ahead of x₁."},"5.9":{"s":"S.H.M. can be drawn as graphs against time. Displacement, velocity and acceleration are all sine or cosine curves. Velocity is a quarter cycle (π/2) ahead of displacement, and acceleration is half a cycle (π) away from displacement.","w":["Velocity is π/2 ahead of displacement; acceleration is π away from displacement."],"x":["Graphs against time show S.H.M. at a glance. The displacement x is a sine or cosine curve between +A and −A. The velocity is also a sine curve, with height Aω, a quarter-cycle ahead of x. The acceleration is a sine curve with height Aω², upside down compared with x.","A good way to read the graph: where x crosses zero, the velocity is at its largest. Where x is at a peak, the velocity is zero and the acceleration is at its most negative.","The kinetic and potential energies, plotted against time, are curves that always stay above zero and have twice the frequency of the motion. Their sum is a flat line at ½kA². Plotted against position, the potential energy is a parabola ½kx², and the kinetic energy is an upside-down parabola, ½k(A² − x²).","For an exam, remember the start. If the body starts from the mean position moving in the positive direction, x = A sinωt. If it starts at the positive extreme, x = A cosωt."],"e":"For x = A cos(ωt), at t = 0 the body is at +A with zero velocity. After T/4 it is at the mean position moving in the negative direction, with speed Aω."},"5.10":{"s":"Two S.H.M.s with the same period along the same line add up to another S.H.M. of the same period. Its amplitude depends on the phase difference: largest (A₁ + A₂) when they are in phase and smallest (the difference) when they are opposite.","f":[{"x":"R = sqrt(A_1^2 + A_2^2 + 2*A_1*A_2*cos(phi_1 - phi_2))","n":"Amplitude of the sum of two S.H.M.s of the same period along one line.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.10, p.120"},{"x":"tan(delta) = (A_1*sin(phi_1) + A_2*sin(phi_2))/(A_1*cos(phi_1) + A_2*cos(phi_2))","n":"Initial phase of the resultant S.H.M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.10, p.120"}],"w":["In phase: R = A₁ + A₂. Opposite phase: R = |A₁ − A₂|. At 90°: R = √(A₁² + A₂²)."],"x":["Sometimes a body takes part in two S.H.M.s at the same time, along the same line. If both have the same angular frequency, the sum is again an S.H.M. with that same frequency, but with a new amplitude and a new phase.","Take x₁ = A₁ sinωt and x₂ = A₂ sin(ωt + φ). The resultant is x = A sin(ωt + δ), with A² = A₁² + A₂² + 2A₁A₂ cosφ. You can get it easily with the reference circle by adding the two rotating arrows (vectors) of length A₁ and A₂.","Two special cases are worth remembering. If φ = 0 (in phase) the amplitudes add: A = A₁ + A₂, the largest possible. If φ = π (opposite phase) they subtract: A = |A₁ − A₂|, the smallest possible, and zero if A₁ = A₂.","This idea returns in waves and in AC circuits, where it explains interference and beats."],"e":"Two S.H.M.s of amplitude 3 cm and 4 cm with a phase difference of π/2 give A = √(9 + 16 + 0) = 5 cm."},"5.11":{"s":"A particle in S.H.M. has kinetic energy because it moves and potential energy because of the restoring force. The kinetic energy is greatest at the mean position and zero at the extremes; the potential energy is the other way round. Their sum is constant, equal to ½mω²A², so it depends on the square of the amplitude. The two energies are equal at x = ±A/√2.","f":[{"x":"E_k = (1/2)*m*omega^2*(A^2 - x^2)","n":"Kinetic energy at displacement x.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"},{"x":"E_p = (1/2)*m*omega^2*x^2","n":"Potential energy at displacement x (= (1/2) k x^2).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"},{"x":"E = (1/2)*m*omega^2*A^2 = (1/2)*k*A^2","n":"Total energy: constant, the same at every position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"},{"x":"E = 2*pi^2*n^2*A^2*m","n":"Total energy in terms of the frequency n.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"}],"w":["Doubling the amplitude makes the total energy four times larger.","At the mean position all the energy is kinetic; at the extremes all of it is potential."],"x":["A particle in S.H.M. has two kinds of energy that keep changing into each other. Its kinetic energy is ½mv² = ½mω²(A² − x²) = ½k(A² − x²). Its potential energy, stored in the restoring force, is ½kx².","The sum is the total energy E = ½kA² = ½mω²A². It is the same at every point of the path, so the energy is conserved when there is no friction.","At the mean position, x = 0: all the energy is kinetic (½kA²). At an extreme, x = ±A: all the energy is potential (½kA²). The two are equal at x = ±A/√2, where each is ¼ kA².","Notice that E depends on the square of the amplitude: if you stretch a spring twice as far, the oscillator carries four times the energy. The average kinetic and potential energies over one cycle are both equal to half the total, ¼ kA² each."],"e":"A block on a spring with k = 100 N/m and A = 0.1 m has E = ½ × 100 × 0.01 = 0.5 J. At x = 0.06 m, PE = ½ × 100 × 0.0036 = 0.18 J and KE = 0.32 J."},"5.12":{"s":"A simple pendulum is a small heavy bob on a light, inextensible string. For small swings its motion is S.H.M. with period T = 2π√(l/g). The period depends on length and g, not on the mass or (for small swings) the amplitude. A seconds pendulum has a period of 2 s.","f":[{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g)","n":"Simple pendulum, small swings; L = length to the centre of the bob.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.12, p.123"},{"x":"L_s = g/pi^2","n":"Length of a seconds pendulum (period 2 s), about 0.99 m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.12, p.123"}],"w":["The period does not depend on the bob's mass, or on the amplitude while it stays small.","A seconds pendulum has a period of 2 s, not 1 s: each one-way swing takes 1 s."],"x":["A simple pendulum is a small heavy bob hung from a light, unstretchable string of length L. Pull it aside by a small angle θ and release it. The weight has a component along the arc, −mg sinθ, that points back to the middle.","For small angles (less than about 10°), sinθ is nearly equal to θ in radians, and θ = x/L. So the restoring force is about −(mg/L)x, proportional to the displacement. The motion is S.H.M., with k = mg/L.","The period is T = 2π√(L/g). It does not depend on the mass of the bob or on the amplitude (when it is small). It grows with the square root of the length, and it is shorter where g is larger.","A pendulum whose period is 2 s is called a second's pendulum. Its length is about 1 m at g = 9.8 m/s². In a lift moving up with acceleration a, the effective gravity is g + a, so the period is shorter. In a freely falling lift g_eff = 0 and the pendulum does not swing. On the Moon (smaller g) a pendulum clock runs slow."],"e":"A pendulum of length 0.64 m with g = 10 m/s² has T = 2π√(0.064) = 1.59 s. Quadruple the length to 2.56 m and T doubles to 3.18 s."},"5.13":{"s":"Angular S.H.M. is oscillation in which the restoring torque is proportional to the angular displacement and opposite to it. Examples are a disc hung on a twisted wire and a magnet turned slightly in a magnetic field. The torque −cθ gives an angular acceleration proportional to −θ, so the angle oscillates in S.H.M. The period depends on the moment of inertia and the torsion constant.","f":[{"x":"tau = -c*theta","n":"Restoring torque on a twisted wire; c = torque per unit angular displacement.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.125"},{"x":"I*d^2(theta)/dt^2 + c*theta = 0","n":"Differential equation of angular S.H.M.; I = moment of inertia.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.125"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(I/c)","n":"Period of angular S.H.M., from T = 2 pi over the square root of the angular acceleration per unit angular displacement.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.125"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(I/(mu*B))","n":"Small oscillations of a magnet of moment mu in a field B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.126"}],"w":["It is S.H.M. only for small angles, where sin θ ≈ θ.","Two magnets joined with like poles together add their moments; with unlike poles together the moments subtract."],"x":["In linear S.H.M. the restoring force is proportional to the displacement. In angular S.H.M. the restoring torque is proportional to the angular displacement: τ = −cθ, where c is the torque per unit angle (the torsion constant). A body that rotates back and forth is a torsion pendulum.","Newton's law for rotation, τ = Iα, gives I d²θ/dt² = −cθ, or d²θ/dt² + (c/I)θ = 0. This has the same form as the equation for linear S.H.M., with θ in place of x, I in place of m and c in place of k.","So the angular frequency is ω = √(c/I) and the period is T = 2π√(I/c). A larger moment of inertia gives a slower oscillation, and a stiffer wire (larger c) gives a faster one.","The balance wheel in a mechanical watch and a disc hung on a wire in the laboratory are examples. A magnet pivoted in the Earth's magnetic field also oscillates this way for small angles."],"e":"A disc with I = 0.02 kg m² hangs on a wire with c = 0.5 N m/rad. T = 2π√(0.02/0.5) = 2π × 0.2 = 1.26 s."},"5.14":{"s":"In a real oscillator, friction or air resistance removes energy, so the amplitude slowly gets smaller. This is damped oscillation. The amplitude falls off exponentially with time.","f":[{"x":"x = A*e^(-b*t/(2*m))*cos(omega'*t + phi)","n":"Damped oscillation; b = damping constant (damping force = -b v).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.14, p.127"},{"x":"omega' = sqrt(k/m - (b/(2*m))^2)","n":"Angular frequency with damping: less than without.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.14, p.127"}],"w":["Damping lowers the frequency a little (longer period) and makes the amplitude fall exponentially."],"x":["In a real oscillator there is always some friction or air resistance. It removes energy at each swing, so the amplitude slowly becomes smaller and in the end the motion stops. Such oscillations are called damped oscillations.","The resisting force is often proportional to the speed, F = −bv. For light damping, the amplitude falls steadily (exponentially) with time, A = A₀e^(−bt/2m), and the period is only slightly longer than the undamped one. The energy, which depends on A², falls too.","If the damping is very heavy, the body returns to the mean position slowly without oscillating at all (overdamped). The case just on the edge of oscillating is critical damping. Good shock absorbers in a car and the pointer of a meter are close to critical damping, so they settle quickly without bouncing.","To keep the oscillation going you must supply energy from outside, as a pendulum clock does with its spring or weights."],"e":"A swing pushed once makes smaller and smaller swings and finally stops. A friend who pushes at the right moments on every swing keeps the amplitude constant."},"5.15":{"s":"Free oscillations happen at the body's own natural frequency. Forced oscillations happen when an outside periodic force drives the body at the driver's frequency. When the driving frequency equals the natural frequency, the amplitude becomes very large. This is resonance, as when soldiers break step on a bridge or a radio is tuned to a station.","f":[{"x":"n = (1/(2*pi))*sqrt(g/l)","n":"Natural frequency of a simple pendulum: it oscillates freely at this frequency.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.15, p.127"}],"w":["Resonance needs the driving frequency to equal the natural frequency, not just a large driving force."],"x":["Free oscillations are those in which the body, after one push, oscillates by itself at its natural frequency, which depends only on the system (for a spring, √(k/m)/2π). They die out in time if there is damping.","Forced oscillations take place when an outside periodic force drives the system. After a short time the body oscillates at the frequency of the driving force, not at its own natural frequency. The amplitude depends on how close the two frequencies are.","When the driving frequency equals the natural frequency, the amplitude becomes very large. This is resonance. Energy is fed in at the best possible rate, in step with the motion, and only damping limits the amplitude. A child on a swing pushed at its natural rhythm swings higher and higher.","Resonance is useful in tuning a radio, in musical instruments and in MRI scanners. It can also be dangerous: soldiers break step on a bridge, and tall buildings are designed so that wind or earthquakes do not drive them at their natural frequency."],"e":"A pendulum of natural frequency 0.5 Hz is driven by a force at 0.5 Hz. The amplitude grows large, but at 1.5 Hz of driving it stays small."}};
